it's great to be joined today by Tom  Nichols who is a professor and also  author of the book the death of  expertise this is a really interesting  topic to me and I'm so glad to have you  on to talk about it the idea of  expertise particularly in the media  world and in the political world has  been an increasingly important topic  really no matter what political issues  one follows give us sort of a general  sense of what you identified as being an  issue within the world of expertise that  led you to write this book in the first  place well the what I initially reacted  to was not people doubting expertise I  mean that's normal if you're a  professional you kind of get used to  that I mean people seek second opinions  from doctors they go get a second  opinion from a lawyer they get multiple  bids from contractors what led me to  write the the initial blog post because  this wasn't even a book it was kind of a  rant was people deciding that they were  smarter than experts and specifically in  one evening people who decided they were  smarter than me about Russia I had a  young person tell me we were talking  about the Edward Snowden business and a  young person said to me tell them that I  don't think you understand Russia now  I'm a Russia expert a Russian speaking  Russian expert had been in the business  for you know 30 years and that finally  kind of you know popped my top when  someone said you know let me explain  Russia to you and this had actually been  germinating for a while I've been  talking up until that point with people  who were experts and things like  intelligence doctors you name it just in  random conversations where they said  yeah you know people keep lecturing me  about what I'm good at and that's what  kind of triggered the idea wasn't that  people doubt experts or have concerns  about expertise it was the experiences  of my experiences along with those of  other experts and professionals who were  really puzzled by people explaining I  suppose you know a lot of women have  said to me this is what man's flaming  feels like but  people explaining your own area of  expertise back to you as though you  don't really understand it yeah and I've  noticed that there a particulate we  don't have to get into them but there  are particular political issues where  this is really prevalent one is  certainly climate change and climate  science and there's a lot of others that  we could talk about it seems that there  is this complexity because you do on the  one hand have a logical fallacy called  the appeal to Authority right suggesting  that something either is right or  couldn't be right based on the perceived  or actual sort of authority of the  person making an assertion but that is  very separate from the reality that  training and education in subjects do  contribute to expertise so how can we  sort of square or or put together those  two sides well one of most important  things about the fallacy of the appeal  to authorities that most people don't  understand it they don't understand that  when someone says look you know you  should listen to me about Russia because  I'm an expert on Russia they say oh well  that's just an appeal to Authority  that's not an appeal to Authority that's  presenting your bona fides appeal to  Authority is saying because I'm you know  a professor you should listen to me  about you know health care or exercise  or something people never understand  this that that they have really become  allergic that when someone says look I'm  a doctor you know you should listen to  me about your heart they say oh that's  an appeal to Authority they think that  it means any authority is an appeal to  Authority and you know it's an irony  that in the death of expertise when  people argue about expertise they don't  even understand the fallacies they're  using and of course if you correct them  then you're being condescending and an  expert and all the things that people  hate I think though the way one way to  square in this problem is that experts  and I think this is one place I could  have improved the book if I may do some  self-criticism is that experts need to  be better about explaining why they're  right I admit that I kind of lose my  patience with people who say well why  should I listen to you because at some  point you do run out of patience with  that it's like yes it'd be like a  student walking into class every day  I know you're the professor and I know  you were hired by Boston College to  teach this course but tell me again why  I should listen to you on the other hand  I think you need to be persuasive and I  think you do need to put out your case  and you do need to be willing to go that  distance as long as people are willing  to listen to you in good faith which i  think is another problem in in the  modern this modern phenomenon of death  for expertise people just aren't willing  to listen how much has basically Google  I guess is what we would call it or the  availability of opinion about just about  any issue on the Internet impacted the  sort of perceived credibility of experts  well I find it fascinating that people  who don't trust New York Times don't  trust the Washington Post don't trust  the Wall Street Journal don't trust  college professors don't trust science  will trust any stray bit of detritus  that goes in front of their screen on  the internet I still argue and I would  still argue now and I argued in the book  that this whole phenomenon predates the  internet I really think this is an  outcome of the late 1960s and relevance  and higher education and therapeutic  models of education child rearing and a  lot of other problems that contributed  to an overall increase in narcissism in  American society but the Internet is an  easy crutch you know because it allows  us to just find basically it allows us  to confirm anything we already believe  and I find that it's I find it amazing  that people who say well I need evidence  and I don't just take the words of  experts will go and type in a search  term in Google and just accept whatever  pops up in further eyes because it's  pretty it's easy to read it's well  presented you know one of the great  curses of the internet is that anything  in 12-point font looks authoritative and  I think that you know that's that is a  real problem is it's not the source of  the depth of expertise but it's really  something that's put it on steroids how  much does this connect to these sort of  pseudo  intellectual movement that's been in the  United States now more and more strongly  over the last decades I've made the case  and reasonable people can disagree but  I've made the case that pseudo  intellectual ISM is in many ways more  dangerous than anti intellectualism once  you have people reading you know Bill  O'Reilly book about the death of Abraham  Lodi the life of Abraham Lincoln or the  death of day of care whatever and  believing that they are scholars of  history for example or reading books by  people like Mark Levin and Laura  Ingraham or whoever that their belief  that they have sort of explored a  variety of viewpoints from experts etc  that that can actually be more dangerous  than just straight out anti  intellectualism I'd be curious to hear  your thoughts and also if and how this  connects to expertise well I I don't I  guess I would part company with you on  the semantics I don't think of you know  Bill O'Reilly books as  pseudo-intellectual I think of them as  anti intellectual I really do so I guess  I should say they are perceived as maybe  intellectual by some of the audience of  for them I mean in a way you know why do  people like Bill O'Reilly books because  they it seems to arm you with knowledge  without actually making you do the work  and I I think you know pseudo  intellectual ism maybe you know this is  just a semantic difference but I don't  really encounter pseudo intellectual ism  as much as I used to say in the 70s or  the 80s people whose people for example  when I think of a pseudo intellectual I  say I think of someone who says oh yes  you know could I find Camus so tiresome  when in fact you know this person has  never read Camus you know or you know I  have some very deep thoughts on  reconstruction and the post slavery you  know period when you know this is not a  person who's ever thought about it  anti-intellectualism says I don't really  need an informed opinion about this my  gut  in fact my opinion is better about this  because I'm just a working job hmm you  know you know why Bill O'Reilly books  are better because he's not a professor  he's not one of you snotty over educated  footnoting kind of intellectuals he's  just an ordinary guy just like you  me and that's why his books are great  and I think that's actually more  dangerous because it's not just junk  it's tasty junk it's it's yummy junk you  know I mean books I I tried reading  killing Reagan because it's an area I  have a lot of knowledge and I Michael I  have some background as a cold war  historian and I you know I was alive  during Reagan I'm working in Washington  and it's crap but it's easy to read crap  so it's not even like that  I guess pseudo-intellectuals lie about  difficult books they can't read anti  intellectuals really do read bad books  and and celebrate it and glory in it and  on speaking of the of textbooks I mean  because you mentioned Bill O'Reilly not  a professor in the book you have a  chapter called higher ed the customer is  always right can you tell us a little  bit about how that factors into this  yeah I think you know college when it  was a natural sorting function for know  let me back up and say college was also  you know class-based experience  wealthier kids went you know not ever  kid that just you know Jonathan any went  to Harvard and he was a C student I mean  this was not always the the province of  bright kids it was off in the province  of rich kids but College throughout the  50s and 60s and into the 70s needs to  serve to sort of natural sorting  function that you know if you went to  college because you were really  interested in going to college now  college is a mass experience we really  do we really have internalized this  belief that everybody should go to  college and the problem is that devalues  a college education because the  competition for those tuition dollars  excuse me the competition for those  tuition dollars really does make  colleges the lower tier colleges not not  not Harvard and Yale I mean Harvard and  Yale our roads can have their  valedictorian but it makes middle and  lower tier colleges have to compete for  students and treat them like clients  rather than students and that means that  you're not going to fail the customer  you're not going to make them  uncomfortable you're not going to be too  challenging you start adverts as I talk  in the book talk about the  you start advertising things like dorms  that our little apartment so you don't  have to pick roommates that you don't  like rock climbing and kayaking and  basically a whole bunch of things that  really have nothing to do with education  when you treat students like clients  you're not treating them like students  College is supposed to be uncomfortable  it's supposed to be where you get  challenged and you know there are times  you should walk out of class really  unhappy but we live in a therapeutic  culture where you know especially in an  educational culture where the students  are always supposed to be happy and  we're always asking them if they're  happy and I think that's really  contributing to the inability to keep  learning even after college and  everything it doesn't mean much now when  a student says well I have a university  education that that may or may not mean  anything anymore but it convinces that  student I'm smart I I don't really need  to learn more than I know and they're  not really equipped to keep learning and  I think that's that's a real tragedy in  Western education we've been speaking  about the book the death of expertise  with the book's author Tom Nichols  thanks so much for talking to us about  this very interesting thanks for having  me

